WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:01.391
- Have you ever been to Cleveland?

00:00:01.391 --> 00:00:05.440
- I went to school very,
very close to Cleveland.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.250
And a good buddy of mine,

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:10.110
was the biggest Cleveland
guy I've ever known.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.157
Rory O'Malley, we went to
college together, we went to

00:00:12.157 --> 00:00:13.380
- Like a big sports guy?

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:14.213
Like.

00:00:14.213 --> 00:00:16.560
- A big sports guy, big
just, big for his city.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:17.393
You know what I'm saying?

00:00:17.393 --> 00:00:18.290
Like just a big.
- I love it.

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:20.670
- Big rep for his city and
we went to college together.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.780
And he, we actually ended up,

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:24.230
we did Hamilton together on Broadway,

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:26.010
he's gonna be the king here,

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.000
In L.A.
- Wow, wow.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.712
- [Leslie] So if you see Hamilton,

00:00:28.712 --> 00:00:29.661
a legacy growing.
- Yes.

00:00:29.661 --> 00:00:30.883
(audience cheering)

00:00:30.883 --> 00:00:31.716
- My God.

00:00:31.716 --> 00:00:34.890
- Do people, do they, I know
you do like many other things,

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:36.220
but do people still freak out?

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.420
Like my thing, is like
everybody comes up and yells,

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:39.253
(yells)

00:00:39.253 --> 00:00:40.086
"Kelly Clarkson."

00:00:40.086 --> 00:00:40.919
Do people?

00:00:40.919 --> 00:00:42.050
This is your Hamilton.
(laughs)

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:44.400
So it's like, do people
always come up to you

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.250
and are just like talking
you non-stop about Hamilton

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:47.520
'cause it was such a movement.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.528
I feel like when I was
a kid and Rent came out.

00:00:49.528 --> 00:00:50.361
- Yeah.

00:00:50.361 --> 00:00:52.140
- How popular that was,
and how it blew up.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.680
That's what Hamilton
is for this generation,

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:55.160
its just like enormous.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.270
- It was that, and it, no
it was weird, you know?

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:00.200
Daveed used to say this thing, this.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.457
I love that Daveed used
to say, "Right outside

00:01:02.457 --> 00:01:05.100
"the stage door you were
a Beatle for a block."

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.008
You know what I mean like?

00:01:06.008 --> 00:01:06.841
(laughing)

00:01:06.841 --> 00:01:08.480
Once you've crossed 8th
avenue it kinda died down

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.680
but it took time to get
used to getting recognized

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:12.513
at the airport,

00:01:12.513 --> 00:01:13.346
and stuff like that.
- Yeah.

00:01:13.346 --> 00:01:14.179
- I was, I'll be looking behind me,

00:01:14.179 --> 00:01:15.330
like who are they looking at?

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:16.163
- Yeah.

00:01:16.163 --> 00:01:19.501
- But it was you know a huge phenomenon,

00:01:19.501 --> 00:01:21.460
changed our lives.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:22.314
- [Kelly] Yeah the
trajectory of your, yeah.

00:01:22.314 --> 00:01:23.147
- [Leslie] Yeah.

00:01:23.147 --> 00:01:26.600
- But it also gave just
really talented people

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.500
a chance an opportunity to really showcase

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:29.333
all that you are capable of.

00:01:29.333 --> 00:01:30.650
Like how he wrote those songs,

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:32.680
I mean, my husband is a full on cowboy.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.633
Like, raises cattle, road
bulls, like that kinda guy.

00:01:35.633 --> 00:01:36.466
- Yeah, yeah.

00:01:36.466 --> 00:01:37.870
- Tears, when we went to New York

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:39.600
and he was like, he was
looking around going,

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:40.720
nobody's looking at me right?

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:43.160
Like, he was like crying.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:44.990
Like it was, it was so good,

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:46.700
just such a great moment in time.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.420
But I'm so glad that
you have the music now,

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:50.980
that you're doing, now.
- Yes.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.140
- I love that you're transitioning.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.740
- So you've done the biggest
(audience applauds)

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:55.560
show on Broadway, literally.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.520
But what was you're first theater role?

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:58.353
Do you remember?

00:01:58.353 --> 00:02:01.230
- My very first theater
role was in kindergarten,

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:02.400
I played Martin Luther King,

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.040
in our black history.
- What a role.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:05.259
- Yes that's right, I started at the top.

00:02:05.259 --> 00:02:06.092
(audience claps)

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:06.925
(laughs)

00:02:06.925 --> 00:02:08.040
- It's all downhill from there.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:11.170
- I know, I played Martin Luther
King in our black history,

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:12.530
you know, ceremony.

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:14.896
And I took it very, very seriously.

00:02:14.896 --> 00:02:18.230
The teacher, Ms. Louis, you know

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:20.570
she was really kind to
the kids, it was like

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:21.700
she wanted you to memorize it,

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.180
but if you didn't it was okay.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.760
She would like sit next
to you and mouth the words

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.030
right before you had to say them.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:27.863
- 'Cause you were like five, right?

00:02:27.863 --> 00:02:28.960
- That's right.
- Yeah.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.842
But I knew all my lines,
all five of my lines.

00:02:31.842 --> 00:02:33.333
- Yes.
(audience cheers)

00:02:33.333 --> 00:02:34.166
(audience claps)

00:02:34.166 --> 00:02:35.590
I have, I retain nothing.

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:36.423
- Yes.

00:02:36.423 --> 00:02:38.410
- This is, just one of the many reasons

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:39.827
I should never be an actor, but yes.

00:02:39.827 --> 00:02:40.710
But I can't retain.

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:42.400
Like how y'all memorize all those lines?

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:43.610
'Cause I did musical theater growing up,

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:46.220
and would almost like, I mean sweaty pits,

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:47.399
my stomach would hurt, like I would,

00:02:47.399 --> 00:02:48.232
(audience laughs)

00:02:48.232 --> 00:02:49.320
Just memorizing that.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.830
- I retain pretty well, I still
might remember some of it,

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:52.682
I mean, I think it

00:02:52.682 --> 00:02:54.120
was something like, you know.
- Wow.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.274
- Martin Luther King had a
dream, something like that

00:02:57.274 --> 00:03:00.600
was pretty much.
(laughs)

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.447
- I think everyone remembers that part.

00:03:02.447 --> 00:03:03.750
- That's right, yeah that's right.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:04.960
- That's amazing, I love you.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:06.690
Okay, was it always
theater for you though?

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:09.070
Or did you, were you
really into TV as well?

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:10.480
- Oh I was a latchkey kid,

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:11.390
so we watched,
- Oh, me too.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:12.643
- I watched, you know?

00:03:12.643 --> 00:03:13.476
- Everything.

00:03:13.476 --> 00:03:14.840
- Far too much TV.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.292
- Me too, but look at us now.

00:03:16.292 --> 00:03:17.755
- I know, you know what I'm saying?

00:03:17.755 --> 00:03:19.533
- They say TV is horrible for you.

00:03:19.533 --> 00:03:20.366
(audience applauds)

00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:22.953
But I think it worked, we're successful.

00:03:22.953 --> 00:03:24.670
We didn't end up hoodlums, it's fine.

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:26.950
- Yeah, I used to watch.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:30.870
Like it's, it was so bad or
good, however you looked at it.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:33.610
Like I, TV sitcoms and stuff

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:35.470
are still a huge comfort to me, like.

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:36.303
- [Kelly] Me too.

00:03:36.303 --> 00:03:37.180
- You see like, when I
go to sleep and stuff,

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:38.013
I can like.
- Nostalgic?

00:03:38.013 --> 00:03:40.590
- Yeah, I can just put on
like an episode of Cheers,

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:41.890
and like, the theme song.
- Yes.

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:42.723
- Like puts me through like this.

00:03:42.723 --> 00:03:43.850
- [Kelly] I know all the theme songs.

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:44.683
- [Leslie] Do you really?

00:03:44.683 --> 00:03:45.610
- Don't you remember like?

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:46.990
I mean, I feel like there was time,

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:49.820
when like every show was
like, the theme song was huge,

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:50.653
For the.
- Had a great theme song.

00:03:50.653 --> 00:03:51.486
- Yes.

00:03:51.486 --> 00:03:52.827
♪ Making your way in the world today ♪

00:03:52.827 --> 00:03:55.080
♪ Takes everything you got ♪

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.030
- Yes, I didn't know all the
words, see I retain nothing.

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:58.200
♪ Taking a break from all your worries ♪

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.270
- But I know Norm,

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:00.820
and I know the whole cast.

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:01.653
(laughs)

00:04:01.653 --> 00:04:02.486
You know my favorite was?
- What's that?

00:04:02.486 --> 00:04:04.271
- Wait, what was the one where

00:04:04.271 --> 00:04:06.600
they had the guy, Urkel?

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:08.697
They had, what was it, Family Matters?

00:04:08.697 --> 00:04:09.530
But what was that song?
- Yeah.

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:11.351
- And then that one lady that went like,

00:04:11.351 --> 00:04:12.323
(sings)

00:04:12.323 --> 00:04:13.156
(laughs)

00:04:13.156 --> 00:04:13.989
Like in that, in the song

00:04:13.989 --> 00:04:14.850
that was my favorite one,

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:16.020
'cause she was just like wailing,

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:17.614
and I was like I wanna be her.

00:04:17.614 --> 00:04:20.180
But 'Mr', your albums called 'Mr'.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:21.380
- [Leslie] Yes.
- Why did you call it 'Mr'?

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:23.300
- That tiny little bit of
mystery, you know what I'm saying?

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:25.480
Like, it has a lot of
meaning to me, but you know.

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:26.420
You can understand this too.

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:27.980
It's like I'm super interested

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:29.240
in people listening to the record

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:31.423
and telling me why they
think it's called 'Mr'.

00:04:31.423 --> 00:04:32.256
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:32.256 --> 00:04:34.310
- It has tones of personal
meaning to me, but I feel like

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:36.126
the minute I say why I called it that.

00:04:36.126 --> 00:04:37.660
- It loses the mystery,

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:39.090
Oh that's kinda cool.
- Exactly.

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:40.680
- I'm not as cool as you, I'm always like,

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:41.513
ah I named it

00:04:41.513 --> 00:04:42.346
(mumbles)

00:04:42.346 --> 00:04:44.024
I need to be cooler, I'm
gonna work on my mystery.

00:04:44.024 --> 00:04:44.857
(laughs)

00:04:44.857 --> 00:04:47.046
I have no filter, I don't
know if you've known this.

00:04:47.046 --> 00:04:48.110
(laughs)

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:51.040
So your wife, daughter and your grandma

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:52.457
were all on this record, right?

00:04:52.457 --> 00:04:53.290
- [Audience] Aw

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:54.123
This is like.
- Yeah.

00:04:54.123 --> 00:04:54.956
- I mean look at, what?

00:04:54.956 --> 00:04:55.789
That's beautiful.
- Well it's,

00:04:55.789 --> 00:04:57.200
you know it's a deeply,

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:59.690
you know its a personal record,
it's like a lot of the songs

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:02.120
started as journal entries, you know.

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:05.974
And so I wanted to, you
know, sew my family into the,

00:05:05.974 --> 00:05:07.020
not literally,

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:08.380
but sew them into the.
- Yeah.

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:09.213
- Fabric of the theme.

00:05:09.213 --> 00:05:10.400
- Very Hannibal Lector, it's fine.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:12.470
- I know yeah, that'd be weird.

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:13.495
It rubs the lotion on your skin.

00:05:13.495 --> 00:05:14.328
- Yeah rub it up
- But it's

00:05:14.328 --> 00:05:16.787
- With the lotion in the basket.

00:05:16.787 --> 00:05:18.940
- But so yeah we, my daughter,

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:20.560
there's a little Easter
egg when my daughter,

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:22.050
introduces one of the tracks.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:24.220
My wife sings back up, I
begged her to sing backup.

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:25.950
She's my favoring singer.
(audience awes)

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:28.500
So my wife sings back up all
over this, all over the album

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:29.333
you can hear her.

00:05:29.333 --> 00:05:32.740
But, yeah my grandmother knew this poem,

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:34.820
from the time when she
was in the 4th grade.

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:37.020
It's a poem called, The Psalm of Life.

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:39.940
And I used to ask her to recite.

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:41.170
She knew it from memory

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:44.110
and I would ask her to
recite it for my whole life,

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:44.943
you know.

00:05:44.943 --> 00:05:48.300
And the meaning sort of changed overtime,

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:49.630
like at first it was just like a gag,

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:51.230
like I wonder if grandma
still remembers it.

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:55.460
And then, as I got older, you
know I started to understand

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:56.910
the meaning of it, and stuff so,

00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:59.240
I had no idea that it
would be the last time,

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:02.680
but that was on the record is a recording,

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:05.575
of the last time my
grandmother ever said that,

00:06:05.575 --> 00:06:06.860
that poem to me.
- You recorded.

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:07.693
- [Leslie] Yeah.

00:06:07.693 --> 00:06:08.526
- You were so smart to do that.

00:06:08.526 --> 00:06:09.359
- Yeah

00:06:09.359 --> 00:06:10.900
- It's so cool, that is the coolest thing.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:12.540
And I think, you know,
we were talking about

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:13.540
you transitioning into like,

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:14.700
more the pop world, or whatever.

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:17.030
And it's like that's the
thing that fans love,

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:18.200
the vulnerability, I think

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:21.000
and the fact that you're
welcoming them into your family,

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:22.270
your story, and it's all right there

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:23.200
and they get to be a part of it.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:24.530
Not just you singing
- Yeah.

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:27.123
- But like welcoming them to
the whole story and opening up.

00:06:27.123 --> 00:06:29.330
- Yeah you, I mean you went
through the same thing, right?

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:32.200
I mean like, when people were
first giving you songs to sing

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:33.033
and telling you what to sing
- Yeah.

00:06:33.033 --> 00:06:35.310
- And then you, you know,
found your voice as a writer.

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:36.143
- Yeah.

00:06:36.143 --> 00:06:36.976
- On your own, that's crazy.

00:06:36.976 --> 00:06:38.880
- I was lucky, I fought very
hard for my first single,

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:41.046
so I got to write on that
one, but it was lucky.

00:06:41.046 --> 00:06:42.143
(audience applauds)

00:06:42.143 --> 00:06:43.078
You cry.

00:06:43.078 --> 00:06:44.286
(laughs)

00:06:44.286 --> 00:06:46.845
It was, we had a really good
relationship with my label.

00:06:46.845 --> 00:06:49.470
But it was, I did have to fight,

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:51.810
but I also know artist, like I'm a writer

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:53.450
and I love writing what's
in my heart and head.

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:55.780
But I also think, like
what a missed opportunity,

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:57.380
for people that maybe don't wanna write

00:06:57.380 --> 00:06:58.460
and do put records out,

00:06:58.460 --> 00:06:59.530
or sing and put records out.

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:01.410
But there these amazing nugget of songs,

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:03.120
that I've been blessed
to be able to record.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.705
Like, that's kinda cool
to, so I like doing both.

00:07:05.705 --> 00:07:06.538
It's fun.

00:07:06.538 --> 00:07:09.120
- Yeah like the greatest compliment to me,

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:12.000
a friend of mine who
I've known a long time,

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:13.080
listened to the record

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:15.260
and the message that he sent to me,

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:17.019
was I feel like I got to know you better

00:07:17.019 --> 00:07:17.852
on this record.
- That's what I'm saying.

00:07:17.852 --> 00:07:19.620
- Somebody that has known
me for a very long time.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:20.621
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:07:20.621 --> 00:07:21.454
- But it's a different thing,

00:07:21.454 --> 00:07:22.620
music opens up a different side of you,

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.950
and its just way more
vulnerable, and it's cool,

00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:26.900
and I wish you nothing but success.

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:27.733
- Thank you so much Kelly.

00:07:27.733 --> 00:07:28.566
Thank you.
- He's so talented,

00:07:28.566 --> 00:07:29.399
so cool, so funny.

00:07:29.399 --> 00:07:31.190
I love that you knew the
lotion in the basket reference.

00:07:31.190 --> 00:07:32.264
(laughs)

